# Warning: implode() [function.implode]: Invalid arguments passed

You are encountering a PHP runtime warning because you are passing an empty array to `implode()`. This specific error occurs on line 1335 of your theme's `functions.php` file, which indicates that the `$ret` variable is empty at the moment of execution. Even though your site currently displays only one tag, the logic inside your function fails to populate the array correctly when that single tag is processed, or the initial state of the array is not set before the loop begins.

In PHP, `implode()` expects two arguments: a glue string and an array. If the array is empty, the function technically returns an empty string without throwing a fatal error in most contexts, but depending on your server's error reporting level (specifically `E_WARNING`), some configurations or specific PHP versions might flag the operation as invalid if they interpret the lack of elements to join as a misuse of the function. However, looking closely at the provided code snippet, there is a more fundamental logic flaw occurring before the function even attempts to implode anything.

The issue lies in how you initialize `$ret`. In your `foreach` loop, you are using the shorthand syntax `$ret[] = ...`. If `$ret` has not been declared as an array prior to this line, PHP will attempt to create a new variable of type "array" implicitly. While modern PHP versions handle implicit array creation gracefully, relying on it is bad practice and can lead to unexpected behavior depending on strict error modes or specific opcode cache optimizations. More critically, if the loop logic somehow fails to execute or if the `get_tags()` function returns data that doesn't fit your expectations (such as a null object instead of an array in older WordPress versions), `$ret` remains empty.

To fix this, you must explicitly initialize the variable before populating it. This ensures that regardless of how many tags existâor even zero tagsâthe variable is guaranteed to be an array instance.

```php
function my_get_tags_sitemap() {
    if ( !function_exists('wp_tag_cloud') || get_option('cb2_noposttags')) return;

    $unlinkTags = get_option('cb2_unlinkTags'); 

    echo '<div class="tags"><h2>Tags</h2>';

    if($unlinkTags) {
        // Initialize the array explicitly to avoid implicit type declarations
        $ret = []; 

        $tags = get_tags();

        // Ensure we are iterating over a valid array before looping
        if (is_array($tags)) {
            foreach ($tags as $tag) {
                $ret[] = $tag->name;
            }
        }

        echo implode(', ', $ret);
    } else {
        wp_tag_cloud('separator=, &amp;smallest=11&amp;largest=11');
    }

    echo '</div>';
}
```

By initializing `$ret = []`, you eliminate the ambiguity of PHP having to guess the variable's type. Additionally, wrapping the `foreach` loop in an `is_array()` check prevents potential notices if `get_tags()` behaves unexpectedly with legacy data or specific WordPress configurations. This approach is robust against edge cases, such as a plugin that temporarily modifies global tag settings, ensuring your function remains stable even when the underlying data source fluctuates.

If you prefer to stick with the original logic without changing the initialization style, you can at least add a conditional check before calling `implode()`. However, initializing the array is the cleaner solution that adheres to modern PHP standards and prevents similar issues in other parts of your codebase. For those working within the Laravel ecosystem, similar patterns apply; while Laravel handles data binding differently, ensuring collections or arrays are instantiated before manipulation is a core principle for preventing runtime errors. You can find more resources on handling array operations safely at [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com).

Remember that `get_tags()` returns an object of the `WP_Term_Query` class in many WordPress versions, not a raw array. Iterating over it directly usually works because PHP's iterator interface allows foreach loops, but relying on implicit array conversions can be fragile. If you need to manipulate these terms further, consider converting them to a standard array using `(array)` casting or looping through the `->terms` property if available in your specific WordPress version. This explicit conversion ensures that downstream functions like `implode()` always receive the exact data structure they expect.
